{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789465827,"nonce":"CmEKi2ZdFXjLlWfzvl8j+8XJv4Y=","title":"Every module-function call goes through the function's cell in both tiers","message":"Cranelift emits a direct `call` to a module function unless the callee is lazy or reloadable, in which case it loads the reload cell (`reload::call_cell_addr(backend, HirId)`) and calls indirect. LLVM emits a module-internal call to the copy it compiled beside the caller. Neither caller sees a callee's later promotion.\n\nAsh's model: one entry table both tiers write and read, every call from compiled code a load of the slot and an indirect call, and a stub value in the slot standing for \"not compiled yet\" (`stub_guarded_call` in ash's cranelift/codegen.rs). Zyntax has the cells already; this makes them the only way compiled code reaches a module function: Cranelift always loads the cell (a lazy function's stub is what the cell holds until its first call), and LLVM declares each callee extern and calls it through the cell too, so an own-module promotion (107df3c) binds nothing by address and picks up every later promotion of its callees.\n\nCost is one load and an indirect call per call the HIR inliner did not absorb; Ash pays the same. Measure on the call kernels (free_function_call, method_call, inlined_call, closure_call) before and after; the ones the inliner absorbs must not move, the rest may lose a nanosecond a call.\n\nDepends on the aggregate convention being shared (the cell is a code address; the caller and callee must agree on the frame). Dropping the direct-call path also removes the `lazy_functions`/reload special cases from the Cranelift call emission.","files":null}]}